473 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 149 0 R
/E (The traditional Mongolian dwelling, known as the ger \(or yurt\), is not merely a tent but a masterwork of sustainable engineering, perfectly adapted to the harsh steppe climate. For over 3,000 years, nomadic herders across Central Asia have relied on the ger as a portable home that can be assembled or dismantled in under an hour. Its structure is deceptively simple: a collapsible wooden lattice wall \(khana\), a crown \(toono\) at )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
474 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 149 0 R
/E (The world reveals its wonders in unexpected places: sometimes in ancient traditions, sometimes in the quiet laws of nature. Here are three stories that bridge culture and science, each offering a glimpse into how deeply human life is intertwined with the natural world.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
475 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 149 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
476 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 149 0 R
/E (From a scientific perspective, the ger is a passive climate control system. The toono serves as both a smoke vent for the stove and a natural air regulator, creating a gentle convection current that circulates fresh air without drafts. Modern architects are now studying ger design for sustainable housing solutions, recognizing that this ancient innovation offers lessons in energy efficiency, portability, and harmony with the environment.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
